WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.994
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.900
The Glass Lab started in
1986, at least the Glass Lab

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:26.940
as we know it today.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:34.390
That's when I started at MIT,
a professor in the Material

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:36.360
Science Department
approached me and said,

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.960
I have a lab in building
4, would you like it?

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.220
And, not knowing what was
in it, I just said yes.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.370
And thinking that I was going
to turn it into a research lab.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.780
But before I had a chance
to even visit the lab,

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:55.110
I was approached by two
students and a glass artist

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:58.920
from outside of MIT to
use the glass furnace

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.580
in the lab that had been there.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.520
It hadn't been
used in many years,

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.284
but they wanted to
turn it on, and I

00:01:05.284 --> 00:01:06.700
didn't know what
glassblowing was,

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:12.390
so I learned with great
difficulty how to blow glass.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:16.950
And, at the same time,
I approached the School

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.470
of Engineering for funding
to do the renovation

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.810
in the laboratory to
turn it into a hot shop

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:25.500
That was at a time when
MIT was particularly

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.730
concerned about innovation
and teaching innovation

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.710
across the campus.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:35.340
And the pitch
became, this is a way

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.890
that students can learn
to improvise together.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:40.980
It's one of the few
craft arts that you

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.010
have to work in a team.

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.680
And one thing led to another.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.360
We began teaching first an
IAP course, and then seminars.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:57.045
And soon it became the most
oversubscribed class at MIT.

00:01:57.045 --> 00:01:59.922
Yeah, so I heard about it at
the beginning of freshman year,

00:01:59.922 --> 00:02:04.315
and I entered in the
lottery over IAP,

00:02:04.315 --> 00:02:07.146
which is during January,
and I didn't get in.

00:02:07.146 --> 00:02:09.414
But I really wanted
to take this class.

00:02:09.414 --> 00:02:11.649
So I signed up for the
lottery again in the spring,

00:02:11.649 --> 00:02:14.720
and I guess I was one of
the lucky few that got it.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:16.220
This is such an
amazing opportunity.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.284
I've been really
interested in glassblowing.

00:02:18.284 --> 00:02:20.644
I knew that coming
here would be something

00:02:20.644 --> 00:02:22.060
that I would want to do.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.892
I definitely want to take
more classes in the future.

00:02:24.892 --> 00:02:27.960
I want to take a couple
of more beginner classes,

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.460
and then maybe move on to
the intermediate level.

00:02:34.834 --> 00:02:38.280
The Glass Lab is a place at MIT
where people can come and use

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:39.590
their hands to do things.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:41.760
And it's one of the
few places where

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.180
you can go and build things
and learn about things that

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:48.330
are part of the curriculum in
some way, but learn about it

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:50.490
the way your hand
and body picks it up,

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:53.340
instead of strictly
intellectually.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:56.670
We encourage a lot
cross-disciplinary play

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:57.510
in the lab.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.870
That's the thing that-- where
the biggest amount of juice

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:03.330
comes out, in my experience,
when students make connections

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:06.570
between using the material
and things that they're

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.136
learning in other situations.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.040
The Glass Lab draws people
from all over the Institute.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:15.410
In fact, it's designed that way.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:18.060
It's a non-credit activity.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:21.150
We've always believed--
if we offer credit,

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:24.630
then only certain majors
will be able to take

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:26.050
advantage of that facility.

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:27.720
So we kept credit
out of the picture,

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:31.080
primarily because it's so
important for students at MIT

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:34.800
to meet people from other
places at the Institute.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.815
And not only meet them,
but work with them.

00:03:37.815 --> 00:03:41.613
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.640
An example of
something unique that's

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.590
come out of the Glass
Lab is the glass band

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:57.410
that's come out this past year.

00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:00.230
[PLAYING GLASS FLUTE]

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:04.175
As a beginner in the
glass blowing program,

00:04:04.175 --> 00:04:07.092
I think I was really excited
to get more involved.

00:04:07.092 --> 00:04:09.300
And music was something that
was very important to me

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:10.470
at the time.

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.980
I'd been playing flute for
10 years at that point.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.040
So I thought it'd be
really exciting to delve

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:18.959
into this project with them.

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:22.350
At the same time,
though, being MIT,

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.240
I felt like we could
push it toward something

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.610
a little bit more exciting.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:28.950
So you can order a
glass flute online.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:31.710
So I thought it would be
much more interesting if we'd

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:34.290
take advantage of the
creative environment

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:37.290
and start working with more
experimental designs, both kind

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:41.640
of experimenting with the
mechanics and the material

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:45.200
interaction with the
voice of the instruments.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:48.480
It festered, or I
should say incubated

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:52.560
for a couple of years, and
then approaching and supporting

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.020
the musical partner
about the idea of,

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:58.200
what if we had this
cross-disciplinary class, where

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.840
we get people from
music department,

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.350
we get somebody who's interested
in composing or helping us put

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:07.830
sounds together so
that we could perform?

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:09.720
Let's find out what
kinds of sounds glass

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:13.980
makes, and make something
happen with that.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.590
So that's how this got started.

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:19.360
But this was a student
inspired course,

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:20.960
if you really think about it.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.160
It wasn't a faculty
member driving us.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.620
It was a student driving us.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:27.150
And that's the
kind of thing that

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:29.870
can happen in a facility
like the Glass Lab.

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:32.344
That's the reason why we
have these things at MIT.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:41.010
Now over 25 years of doing
it, we've seen just the impact

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:45.270
that that's had on graduates.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:49.080
They look back on the
Glass Lab experience at MIT

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:52.140
as some of the most
important activities

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:55.700
that they've done, primarily
because of who they met

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:57.600
and who they've worked with.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:02.460
Most do not become glass artists
when they take this course.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.320
But they have an
experience trying

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:09.900
to improvise with people of all
different types of backgrounds

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:11.920
and capabilities.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:14.400
And that's good because
everybody's learning

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.870
as we do these courses.

